// Text output (M6 Task 10).
//
// `print`, `println` and `read_line` are builtins: they are in scope in every
// file, imported or not, because Yashy's modules share one root scope (the
// plan's Task 9 rule). `println` already takes one value of any printable type,
// so a module-level `print` or `println` would never be reached by that name;
// the compiler rewrites both before ordinary name resolution. What this module
// adds is the explicitly typed number forms, for a call site that wants the
// conversion named.
//
// The file half of I/O is built in too: `read_file(path)` answers with the whole
// file (empty on failure), and `write_file(path, body)` answers 1 or 0. They are
// documented here because this is the module a reader looks in for them; there is
// no wrapper, because a wrapper would have to use the same name and the names
// live in one scope.
//
// Ownership: none of these consume their argument. `println` copies the bytes
// and keeps no pointer.

// Ownership: none; a number owns nothing.
pub fn println_i64(value: int) {
    println(value);
}

// Ownership: none; a number owns nothing.
pub fn println_f64(value: float) {
    println(value);
}
